@page "/projects/seeding"

<ProjectPage Title="Database seeding system"
             Kicker="Professional work · 2025–26"
             AccentClass="accent-green"
             Summary="A modular seeding system for an education platform, giving every developer reproducible test data across environments and feeding the automated integration tests."
             Tech="@(new[] { "JavaScript", "Express.js", "MongoDB", "Mongoose", "Faker", "AJV", "Lodash" })">

    <h2>What it is</h2>

    <p>
        Built at <strong>Klarr Utvecklingsbyrå</strong> for an education platform, and
        the subject of my thesis. Developers needed realistic data to work against and the
        integration tests needed a known starting state — and both were being solved
        ad hoc, differently, by each person.
    </p>

    <p>
        The system generates that data on demand: configurable volumes, the relationships
        between entities, and the awkward edge cases that only show up in real use. It
        supports both <strong>full and partial seeding</strong>, so you can rebuild
        everything or top up one collection without wiping your working state.
    </p>

    <h2>Designed for people who aren't me</h2>

    <p>
        A seeding script is easy. A seeding <em>system</em> that other developers will
        actually use, extend, and not resent is the harder problem, and that's what
        shaped the design.
    </p>

    <p>
        It's modular, so adding a new entity means writing one generator rather than
        touching a monolith. Volumes and relationships are configurable rather than
        hardcoded, because the data you want for a UI demo isn't the data you want for a
        performance check. Generated documents are validated against schemas with
        <strong>AJV</strong>, so the system can't quietly produce records the application
        would reject.
    </p>

    <h2>Finding out what was actually needed</h2>

    <p>
        Before building it I gathered requirements from the people who'd depend on it —
        developers with different workflows, the automated test suite with its own
        constraints, and the platform's future direction. Those wants conflicted:
        <strong>fast, small datasets</strong> for day-to-day work against
        <strong>large, realistic ones</strong> for meaningful testing.
    </p>

    <p>
        Configurability was the resolution rather than a compromise on either side. That
        work — balancing competing priorities across teams into one design — was the part
        I found most valuable, and it's what the thesis examined.
    </p>

    <h2>Integrated, documented, handed over</h2>

    <p>
        The system plugs into the automated test suite so integration tests run against a
        known, reproducible state instead of whatever happened to be in the database.
    </p>

    <p>
        I wrote onboarding documentation and presented the architecture to the development
        team. That mattered as much as the code: a tool nobody understands gets worked
        around, and a system only one person can extend becomes that person's permanent
        obligation.
    </p>

</ProjectPage>
